    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lukeni-lua-nimi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lukeni-lua-nimi.jpg" alt="Lukeni lua Nimi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lukeni-lua-nimi">Lukeni lua Nimi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lukeni_Lua_Nimi-2_(1)_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms. Milora Lipscomb, Mr. Bruce Mateso and Paari-editeur, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Kongo emerged in the late fourteenth century in the region south of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, consolidating a series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> communities into a centralised polity. Its foundation is traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lukeni-lua-nimi">Lukeni lua Nimi</a>, who established the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a> and unified territories through conquest and alliance. The kingdom's rise was facilitated by fertile agricultural land, access to riverine trade routes and its position between the Atlantic coast and the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>. By the late fifteenth century it had expanded to encompass provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>, Mbamba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpemba">Mpemba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbata">Mbata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsundi">Nsundi</a> under the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikongo">manikongo</a>, or king. A pivotal moment came in 1483 with the arrival of the Portuguese navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> at the mouth of the Congo, which opened diplomatic and commercial relations that would profoundly shape Kongo's political trajectory. The baptism of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nzinga-a-nkuwu">Nzinga a Nkuwu</a> as João I in 1491 marked the beginning of a new ideological framework for royal government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kongo society was hierarchical, organised around kinship and provincial administration. It was divided into nobles, commoners and enslaved people, with lineage central to status and political influence. Agriculture formed the economic foundation, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, and later the American crops <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, supporting both subsistence and surplus production. The kingdom used nzimbu shells, gathered on the royal fishery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda-island">Luanda Island</a>, as currency, alongside squares of finely woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raffia-cloth">raffia cloth</a>. Integration into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> transformed the economy during the sixteenth century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, raffia cloth and, above all, enslaved people were exchanged for European textiles, metalware and later firearms. Markets operated at local and regional levels, moving goods across provinces. Revenue came through tribute, with provincial governors remitting goods and labour to the court. The growing Atlantic demand for slaves intensified social stratification and caused demographic disruption, as warfare and raiding were increasingly directed towards capturing people for export.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila.jpg" alt="Battle of Mbwila" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KingdomKongo1711.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kongo's military organisation combined provincial levies with royal forces, mobilised under the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikongo">manikongo</a>. Armies consisted of infantry armed with bows, spears, swords and shields; cavalry played no role, as tsetse fly made horses impractical. After contact with the Portuguese, firearms were introduced, though their distribution remained uneven and often controlled by elite factions, and Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> sometimes served in royal armies. Campaigns were essential to the kingdom's expansion and consolidation, particularly during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> (r. 1509–1543), who won the throne by defeating his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpanzu-a-kitima">Mpanzu a Kitima</a> and then sought to strengthen central authority and suppress rebellion. The kingdom also fought neighbouring states and rival claimants to the throne, and in 1568 invaders known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaga">Jaga</a> briefly drove King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-i-of-kongo">Álvaro I</a> from his capital until Portuguese troops helped restore him. A critical turning point came in 1665 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a>, where Kongo forces confronted Portuguese troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and their African allies. The defeat, in which King António I was killed and beheaded, fragmented royal authority and exposed a military system dependent on internal cohesion it no longer possessed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Afonso I of Kongo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I of Kongo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manikongo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Affricoll9, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Governance in Kongo combined a centralised monarchy with a network of provinces governed by appointed officials, usually drawn from noble lineages. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikongo">manikongo</a> ruled with a royal council and controlled key administrative and judicial functions, while provincial governors handled local administration, taxation and military mobilisation and could be recalled by the court. Because the throne was elective among descendants of earlier kings rather than strictly hereditary, each succession invited competition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> introduced new legal and ideological elements, including written correspondence, diplomatic protocol and ecclesiastical structures, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a> was renamed São <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> sought to formalise administration along Christian lines, establishing schools and promoting literacy, and in 1526 wrote to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">João III of Portugal</a> protesting the abuses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Customary law nonetheless remained important, particularly in rural areas. Without a fully institutionalised bureaucracy, central control depended on personal alliances and the loyalty of provincial elites, a flexible arrangement that proved vulnerable during succession disputes and external pressure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Kongo occupied a central position in the early modern Atlantic world, serving as a key intermediary between the societies of the interior and European maritime powers. Its diplomatic engagement with Portugal was among the earliest sustained relationships between a sub-Saharan African state and a European kingdom, and it extended to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrique-of-kongo">Henrique</a> was consecrated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1518, and Kongo envoys reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the early seventeenth century. The adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the use of Portuguese in royal correspondence and the integration of European goods into local economies illustrate the depth of this interaction. Regionally, Kongo exerted influence over neighbouring polities through trade, warfare and alliance. Its participation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> had profound consequences within its own society and across the Atlantic, as enslaved people from Kongo became a major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Kongo religious and cultural elements persist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, reflecting its enduring global impact.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Kingdom of Kongo was a protracted process driven by internal fragmentation and external pressure. The intensification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> aggravated internal conflicts, as rival factions competed for control of trade routes and access to European goods, while the founding of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in 1576 created a hostile neighbour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a> in 1665 marked a decisive rupture, and in its aftermath the kingdom descended into civil war, with rival royal houses contesting the throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">São Salvador</a> abandoned, and provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a> asserting independence. Economic disruption, population loss and political fragmentation eroded the kingdom's capacity to function as a cohesive state. The prophetic Antonian movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimpa-vita">Beatriz Kimpa Vita</a> sought to restore the kingdom before her execution in 1706, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-iv-of-kongo">Pedro IV</a> reoccupied São <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> and nominally reunified the kingdom in 1709. Yet real power remained with provincial lords, and Kongo never regained its former prominence; it survived as a diminished monarchy, increasingly under Portuguese control, until the title lost all political meaning in the early twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Kongo stands among the first sub-Saharan African states to sustain diplomatic relations with a European kingdom, adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Portuguese literacy while its growing entanglement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> fed the internal conflicts that broke it apart after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a> in 1665.</em></p>

